Starter code:

class Solution:
    def countGoodArrays(self, n: int, m: int, k: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
This is a standard combinatorics problem. Choose which k of the n−1 adjacent pairs are equal: C(n−1, k). The first element has m choices. Each of the n−1−k "different" adjacent positions has (m−1) choices, since it must differ from the previous element. Total = m · C(n−1, k) · (m−1)^(n−1−k) mod p.

Check: n=3, m=2, k=1 → 2 · C(2,1) · 1^1 = 4 ✓. n=4, m=2, k=2 → 2 · C(3,2) · 1^1 = 6 ✓. n=5, m=2, k=0 → 2 · 1 · 1 = 2 ✓.

I checked this against all three examples by hand before submitting.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "T-4531", "code": "class Solution:\n    def countGoodArrays(self, n: int, m: int, k: int) -> int:\n        MOD = 10**9 + 7\n        # C(n-1, k) via factorials\n        N = n - 1\n        num = 1\n        den = 1\n        for i in range(k):\n            num = num * (N - i) % MOD\n            den = den * (i + 1) % MOD\n        comb = num * pow(den, MOD - 2, MOD) % MOD\n        return m % MOD * comb % MOD * pow(m - 1, n - 1 - k, MOD) % MOD\n"}

