=== MODEL ===
This is a max-min selection problem, so the standard approach is binary search on the answer d, plus a feasibility check: can we pick k points with pairwise Manhattan distance ≥ d?

Key facts for the boundary: map each boundary point to a 1D perimeter coordinate t in [0, 4·side). Manhattan distance between two boundary points is not simply the perimeter distance, so the feasibility check needs care. I'll do the check with a greedy on the sorted perimeter order: start from some point, repeatedly take the next point whose pairwise distance to all chosen points is ≥ d, and check whether we reach k. For a fixed starting point the greedy is the usual interval-packing argument, and trying each starting point gives a correct but O(P²·k) check per binary step.

With P up to 15,000 and k up to 25, that's roughly 15000² · 25 per check, far too slow. I'm flagging this clearly: I have not found the efficient version, and this submission would likely time out on large inputs. I am submitting it anyway because I have no better candidate yet, and I'd rather report that risk than present it as final.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "T-1020", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxDistance(self, side: int, points: List[List[int]], k: int) -> int:\n        def md(a, b):\n            return abs(a[0] - b[0]) + abs(a[1] - b[1])\n\n        def perim(p):\n            x, y = p\n            if y == 0:\n                return x\n            if x == side:\n                return side + y\n            if y == side:\n                return 3 * side - x\n            return 4 * side - y  # x == 0\n\n        pts = sorted(points, key=perim)\n        P = len(pts)\n\n        def feasible(d):\n            # try every starting point, greedily pick points in perimeter order\n            for s in range(P):\n                chosen = [pts[s]]\n                for j in range(P):\n                    if j == s:\n                        continue\n                    ok = True\n                    for c in chosen:\n                        if md(c, pts[j]) < d:\n                            ok = False\n                            break\n                    if ok:\n                        chosen.append(pts[j])\n                        if len(chosen) >= k:\n                            return True\n            return False\n\n        lo, hi = 0, 2 * side\n        while lo < hi:\n            mid = (lo + hi + 1) // 2\n            if feasible(mid):\n                lo = mid\n            else:\n                hi = mid - 1\n        return lo\n"}

