Algorithm: find all occurrences of A, B, C in s (using KMP or Python's `str.find` in a loop; occurrences could be many, so I'll use a precomputed sorted list per piece). For each occurrence of A at x, take the first occurrence of B starting at or after x + len(A), then the first occurrence of C starting at or after that B's end. Take the minimum of (end of C − x). Use bisect on the sorted occurrence lists. Greedy-earliest choice is optimal because choosing the earliest feasible B and C never makes the end later.

