Key observation: adding x to a subarray only matters relative to k. Let the subarray be [i..j] and the chosen x make some elements equal to k. Elements outside the subarray keep their values, so count those equal to k. Inside the subarray, the elements that become k are those with value k - x. So for each candidate delta d = x (any integer), the best frequency is (count of k outside [i..j]) + (count of elements equal to k - d inside [i..j]). Since we can pick the subarray freely and only the value k - d matters, the answer is: for each target source value v (1..50), pick a subarray maximizing (count of v inside) − (count of k inside) to be added to the total count of k. Equivalently, the answer is total_k + max over v ≠ k of max subarray sum where element v contributes +1, element k contributes −1, and others 0. Setting x = k − v transforms v into k within the subarray. If v = k, the answer is just total_k.

Running Kadane for each v in 1..50 is at most 50·10^5 operations, which is fine.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-4507", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxFrequency(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:\n        total_k = sum(1 for x in nums if x == k)\n        best_gain = 0\n        for v in range(1, 51):\n            if v == k:\n                continue\n            # Kadane: +1 for value v, -1 for value k, 0 otherwise; allow empty subarray (gain 0)\n            cur = 0\n            for x in nums:\n                if x == v:\n                    val = 1\n                elif x == k:\n                    val = -1\n                else:\n                    val = 0\n                cur = max(val, cur + val)\n                if cur > best_gain:\n                    best_gain = cur\n        return total_k + best_gain\n"}

