Approach: fix the middle pair (q, r) with r - q > 1. For each q, count p < q-1 by value, and for each r, count s > r+1 by value. The condition nums[p]*nums[r] == nums[q]*nums[s] means nums[p]/nums[q] == nums[s]/nums[r], so I'll count by reduced fractions. Iterating r from left to right with a counter of s-values in the suffix, and a counter of p-ratios maintained as q advances, gives an O(n²) solution with gcd reduction. I'll check Example 1 by hand after writing it: for (0,2,4,6), ratio nums[0]/nums[2] = 1/3 and nums[6]/nums[4] = 1/3, so it matches.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-8357", "code": "from typing import List\nfrom math import gcd\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nclass Solution:\n    def numberOfSubsequences(self, nums: List[int]) -> int:\n        n = len(nums)\n        # suffix counter of fractions nums[s]/nums[r] for s >= r+2, built per r\n        total = 0\n        for q in range(1, n):\n            # p ranges over indices <= q-2; ratio nums[p]/nums[q]\n            left = Counter()\n            for p in range(q - 1):\n                g = gcd(nums[p], nums[q])\n                left[(nums[p] // g, nums[q] // g)] += 1\n            for r in range(q + 2, n - 1):\n                # need nums[p]*nums[r] == nums[q]*nums[s]  =>  nums[p]/nums[q] == nums[s]/nums[r]\n                # count s in [r+2, n-1] with nums[s]/nums[r] == nums[p]/nums[q]\n                right = Counter()\n                for s in range(r + 2, n):\n                    g = gcd(nums[s], nums[r])\n                    right[(nums[s] // g, nums[r] // g)] += 1\n                for key, cnt in left.items():\n                    total += cnt * right.get(key, 0)\n        return total\n"}

