For n up to 10^5 a naive O(n²) scan is about 10^10 in the worst case, which is too slow in Python. I'll use a segment tree over baskets storing max capacity among unused ones, so each query finds the leftmost index with capacity ≥ fruit in O(log n).
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-1251", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def numOfUnplacedFruits(self, fruits: List[int], baskets: List[int]) -> int:\n        n = len(baskets)\n        size = 1\n        while size < n:\n            size *= 2\n        # segment tree of max capacity over unused baskets; unused leaves beyond n are 0\n        tree = [0] * (2 * size)\n        for i in range(n):\n            tree[size + i] = baskets[i]\n        for i in range(size - 1, 0, -1):\n            tree[i] = max(tree[2 * i], tree[2 * i + 1])\n\n        def place(x):\n            # find leftmost leaf with value >= x; return its index or -1\n            if tree[1] < x:\n                return -1\n            node = 1\n            while node < size:\n                if tree[2 * node] >= x:\n                    node = 2 * node\n                else:\n                    node = 2 * node + 1\n            idx = node - size\n            tree[node] = 0\n            node //= 2\n            while node >= 1:\n                tree[node] = max(tree[2 * node], tree[2 * node + 1])\n                node //= 2\n            return idx\n\n        unplaced = 0\n        for f in fruits:\n            if place(f) == -1:\n                unplaced += 1\n        return unplaced\n"}

