    def minTime(self, skill: List[int], mana: List[int]) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
This is the classic "Calculate Minimum Time to Brew Potions" problem. The known approach: for consecutive potions j-1 and j, the start of potion j is determined by the maximum over wizards of (prefix-sum of skill up to i) * mana[j-1] - (prefix-sum up to i-1) * mana[j], plus the previous start. Total time is the start of the last potion plus the sum of all skill times times the last mana value.

Concretely, let P[i] = skill[0]+...+skill[i-1] (prefix sums, P[0]=0). For each adjacent pair (a=mana[j-1], b=mana[j]), the required gap is max over i in 1..n of (P[i]*a - P[i-1]*b) — the gap must ensure that every wizard is free when the potion arrives. The answer is sum of gaps plus P[n]*mana[-1].

Checking example 2: skill=[1,1,1], mana=[1,1,1]. P=[0,1,2,3]. Pair (1,1): max over i of (P[i]-P[i-1]) = 1. Two pairs give gap 2, plus P[3]*1 = 3, total 5. Correct.

Checking example 3: skill=[1,2,3,4], mana=[1,2]. P=[0,1,3,6,10]. Pair (1,2): max over i of P[i]*1 - P[i-1]*2 = max(1-0, 3-2, 6-6, 10-12) = 1. Total = 1 + P[4]*2 = 1 + 20 = 21. Correct.

I'll submit this solution to T-8662.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "T-8662", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def minTime(self, skill: List[int], mana: List[int]) -> int:\n        n = len(skill)\n        P = [0] * (n + 1)\n        for i in range(n):\n            P[i + 1] = P[i] + skill[i]\n        total = 0\n        for j in range(1, len(mana)):\n            a = mana[j - 1]\n            b = mana[j]\n            gap = max(P[i] * a - P[i - 1] * b for i in range(1, n + 1))\n            total += gap\n        total += P[n] * mana[-1]\n        return total\n"}

